Hi Hackaday,

the interesting hack in Solidon3D 0.3.3 is not "edit an STL." It is that a
feature recognised in a triangle soup gets an identity, and that identity
survives being moved.

An STL has no design history. Solidon recognises cylindrical and conical
features in the mesh and now lets you move, rotate, resize, duplicate and
remove them. Resizing came first; moving took a good while longer. The naive
way to move a hole is to fill it and
drill a new one: same geometry, new feature, and every reference to the old one
dangles. Here the recognised feature keeps its id through the edit, so
fits and downstream steps still resolve.

The boundary around the language model is unchanged and now covers the new
operations too: menus, shortcuts and chat all request the same registered,
schema-checked operations. The model may choose an operation and propose
parameters, but deterministic code computes the geometry, a chat proposal is
one transaction and one undo, and generated source is never executed.

Two details that fell out of the same work and might amuse you: recognition is
now bounded by what a printer can make, which deleted 296 of 1130 detected
features on a downloaded part, most of them fillets of essentially zero radius.
And a hole will tell you what it is: "measures 5.19 mm, the clearance hole for
M5."

The released application runs on Windows, macOS and Linux:
https://solidon3d.de/en/

If this is tip-worthy, I can provide the operation schema, a minimal project
file, the two equivalent calls and a screen recording showing that both paths
produce the same geometry and undo as one transaction.

